/*
  core/stressors/time_pressure_deadline.spthy -- f_U for sigma3 TimePressure, v2
  (DEADLINE-CONTEXT-triggered), CEREMONY-AGNOSTIC.

  The v1 detector (time_pressure.spthy, kept for alex_blake_kdf) reads the lexicon's
  TemporalDemand row -- time pressure as a property of the ACTION CLASS. This v2 detector is
  closer to the literature: time pressure is SITUATIONAL, a constraint the environment/interface
  imposes on a step, not something intrinsic to computing or sharing (Maule & Svenson 1993,
  time-pressure research; NASA-TLX Temporal Demand is likewise rated per situation, Hart &
  Staveland 1988). So the trigger reads:

    * !Step(P, pid, action)   -- Layer 1: the interface's prompt
    * !UnderDeadline(pid)     -- Layer-1 CONTEXT: the interface presented THIS control under a
                                 timer (an expiring secure link, a send-before deadline). Emitted
                                 by the interface alongside the prompt -- the stressor layer still
                                 reads only the interface, never the protocol.

  No lexicon row and no task name: which controls are time-boxed is an interface design fact,
  exactly like a !Demands row is an interface's demand profile. At onset it emits SetMask(P,'Busy')
  -- everything below SetMask is UNCHANGED. !StressEnable(P) gates per party; OneTimePressure
  bounds the onset (same name as v1's restriction -- the two sigma3 files are never co-included).
*/

rule Trigger_TimePressureDeadline [color=#eb5757]:
    [ !Step(P, pid, action),
      !UnderDeadline(pid),
      !StressEnable(P) ]
  --[ TimePressure(P), SetMask(P,'Busy') ]->
    [ ]

restriction OneTimePressure:
  "All p #i #j. TimePressure(p)@i & TimePressure(p)@j ==> #i = #j"
